People are often surprised when I tell them that my hardest postpartum was my third.

By then I had done it twice. I knew how to hold a newborn, how to change a diaper in the dark, how the first weeks tend to go. If experience could protect anyone, you would think it would have protected me.

It did not. And I have come to believe that this is one of the most important things I know about my work.

## Every season is its own

I have experienced postpartum three times, and each season taught me something different about motherhood, about recovery, and about the kind of support we need along the way.

That sounds simple. It is also very easy to forget when you are in the middle of it.

A new baby does not arrive into the same life twice. The body is different, the family is different, the season around it is different. What you learned last time is real, and it is also not a map for this time.

I am not going to write down every detail of my third postpartum here. Some of it belongs to my family, and some of it is still settling into words. What I can tell you is that it was difficult, and that I went looking for the support and resources I needed to find my way through it.

Looking for help was not a sign that something was wrong with me. It was the most honest thing I could do.

## What I was looking for in the middle of the night

A lot of that searching happened in the middle of the night.

I wanted information that felt calm, honest, and grounded in real life. I did not want another voice telling me what I should already be doing. I did not want advice that made me feel like I was falling behind, as if there were a correct version of postpartum and I had somehow missed it.

What I needed, more than any single answer, was a steadier tone. Something that said: this is common, here is what you can try, here is who to call if it does not get better, and you are not failing.

I often think about how many other mothers are awake at that same hour, reading the same things and feeling the same pressure. I do not think I was the only one, and I know you are not.

The Cora Journal started there. It is my attempt to write the kind of thing I was looking for.

## How it changed the way I work

That season changed the way I see postpartum care. It deepened something that was already in me, a desire to make sure other mothers do not feel they have to go through this alone.

It shaped almost everything about how Cora works today.

It is why I care so much about preparation. I want families to plan for postpartum before they are in it, while there is still time, sleep and energy to think clearly. Not to make it perfect, but so that fewer things have to be figured out at 3 a.m.

It is why the mother is at the center of my care. The baby matters deeply, and so does the person who is recovering, learning, feeding, healing and holding so much. In the hardest weeks, it is very easy for everyone to look only at the baby.

It is why I do not push one right way. Breast, bottle, formula, a mix of both, more rest, more help, a different rhythm than you expected. My job is to give you calm guidance and support your choices, not to add another set of rules.

And it is why I try to be the calm person in the room. The one who can offer practical help, answer questions without judgment, encourage rest, and remind you that needing care does not mean you are failing.

## If you are in your own hard season

If this is not your first baby and it is harder than you expected, I want you to know that this happens. {% sublinhado %}Having done it before does not mean you should be able to do it alone.{% /sublinhado %}

If this is your first, and you are already worried about being behind, I want you to know that there is no schedule you are late for.

And if you are reading this in the middle of the night, looking for something calm, I am glad you found your way here. Please do not carry the hard parts in silence.

Tell someone how you are really doing, and if something feels off with your body or your mood, reach out to your OB, your midwife or your doctor. You do not have to be sure it is serious to ask.

Looking back, I do not think the mother I was during those nights needed to be more experienced. She needed someone to ask her how she was.

So let me ask you, since you are here: how are you, really?

You deserve to be cared for, too.
